\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Corollary}
\title{Invariant observables and normalized reduced sums}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{For the \vocabk{orbit gauge–Higgs weight}{fdip-010A} over a
commutative unital ring #{k}, a joint observable with values in a
#{k}-module is invariant precisely when it is a function of the
residual orbit of the reduced connection. For such an invariant
observable #{f}, let #{\check f(V)} be its restriction to the
constant scalar #{\phi^\circ}. Then
##{\sum_{U,\phi}\Xi(U,\phi)f(U,\phi)
 = |\mathcal O|^{|X|}\sum_V\check\Xi(V)\check f(V).}
When #{Z} is a unit the normalized joint value equals the
reduced value normalized by #{\check Z}.}

\proof{
\p{An invariant function is constant on each joint orbit. Use the
bijection in \ref{fdip-0112} to obtain a function on residual
orbits. Conversely pulling such a function back is invariant.
The change of variables in \ref{fdip-010Y} gives the numerator
identity since both the weight and observable at the constant
scalar depend only on #{V}. If #{Z} is a unit, its factors
#{|\mathcal O|^{|X|}} and #{\check Z} are units in #{k}, which
permits division.}

\p{For example, over #{\mathbb F_2} let the group with two elements
interchange the basis vectors of #{\mathbb F_2^2}. Take their
free orbit on one vertex without edges, with site weight one.
Then #{\check Z=1} and #{Z=0}. The reduced value can be normalized,
while the joint quotient has zero denominator.}
}
